# The clipboard scene: one clip in several representations, and the
# privileged read that takes one back (DESIGN.md, Clipboard).
#
# EVERY ASSERTION HERE CROSSES A PROCESS BOUNDARY. What the guest copies
# is read back by the platform's own tools, and what the guest reads was
# put there by them — `pbcopy`/`pbpaste`/`osascript`/`sips` on mac,
# wl-copy/wl-paste on wayland, the helper app on android. That is not
# thoroughness for its own sake: kaya's representation set is closed
# because the LOWERINGS are the hard part (CF_HTML's mandatory offset
# header, Android's content:// URI for an image, CF_HDROP's struct), and
# a check where kaya reads what kaya wrote parses its own malformed
# header perfectly happily. It would be a check that cannot fail for the
# reason the design exists.
#
# THE FILES ARE THE GUEST'S OWN, written before anything is shown, at
# <scene root>/kaya-clip-<pid>/. Guest and interpreter agree on that
# path with no runner involvement, and the pid keeps parallel legs from
# colliding.
expect label#0 "ready"

# ---- copying: four representations, one clip ----------------------
#
# The consumer takes the richest it understands, which is why an app
# offers html AND text rather than making kaya derive one from the
# other. All four come off the same clip.
click button#0
expect label#0 "copied"
expect_clipboard text "kaya clip"
expect_clipboard html "<b>kaya</b> clip"
expect_clipboard dev.kaya.note "note=1"

# THE IMAGE IS A DECODED SIZE, never a byte count: every host
# re-encodes freely between image types — macOS synthesizes tiff, jpeg,
# gif and four more from one png on demand — so bytes would be a
# different number on every lane for the same picture.
expect_clipboard image "4x4"

# ---- the custom format, read back ---------------------------------
#
# THE ONE THING NO FOREIGN PROCESS CAN SEED, and deliberately so: no
# stock tool on any platform writes an app-defined type, and a helper
# kaya wrote would be foreign in name only. Its whole specification is
# that it round-trips within the app and that kaya does nothing clever
# with the bytes — and the foreign read above already confirmed the
# bytes really are on the clipboard under that id.
click button#1
expect label#0 "custom dev.kaya.note note=1"

# ---- reading what another app put there ---------------------------
#
# The privileged read, answering content this process did not write.
clipboard_seed text "from another app"
click button#2
expect label#0 "text from another app"

# THE READ IS FILTERED BY WHAT IT ACCEPTS. Nothing but text is on the
# clipboard now, and this read accepts only files — so the answer is
# EMPTY, which is the same answer a denied prompt or an unfocused
# reader gives. The guest does not try to tell them apart because no
# platform says which.
click button#4
expect label#0 "empty"

# ---- an image, in and straight back out ---------------------------
#
# The seeded picture arrives as bytes, and the guest copies it back so a
# foreign DECODER can say what it is. A backend that mangled the image
# on either leg fails here rather than on a byte count nobody can
# compare across platforms.
clipboard_seed image "$TMP/kaya-clip-$PID/pixel.png"
click button#3
expect label#0 "image"
expect_clipboard image "4x4"

# ---- a pasted file, opened for real -------------------------------
#
# ALL THE WAY TO THE BYTES, the filedialog scene's claim in the other
# direction: a file on the clipboard is the SAME capability the picker
# returns, so the guest redeems the handle and reads it with its own
# file API — off the app thread, because `open` blocks. The line below
# fails unless a real descriptor came back carrying the real file.
clipboard_seed files "$TMP/kaya-clip-$PID/pasted.txt"
click button#4
expect label#0 "files pasted.txt pasted bytes"

# One REAL-TREE read at the end: every assertion above reads kaya's own
# model or the clipboard, and would pass for an arm that ran and drew
# nothing.
expect_ax label#0 "label/files pasted.txt pasted bytes"
